  "response_text": " I'm so sorry to hear you're experiencing stress from discrimination at work. Here are some warning signs to watch for and ways to build a support network:\n\n**Warning Signs:**\n\n1.  **Increased anxiety and stress**: Feeling overwhelmed, on edge, or constantly worried about your job or well-being.\n2.  **Physical symptoms**: Headaches, stomach problems, or other physical complaints that may be related to stress.\n3.  **Changes in behavior**: Avoiding social interactions, becoming withdrawn, or exhibiting irritability.\n4.  **Decreased productivity**: Struggling to complete tasks, making mistakes, or feeling unfulfilled in your work.\n5.  **Emotional exhaustion**: Feeling drained, depleted, or hopeless.\n\n**Building a Support Network:**\n\n1.  **Talk to a trusted friend or family member**: Share your experiences and feelings with someone you trust.\n2.  **Seek support from a mental health professional**: Consider therapy or counseling to address your emotional well-being.\n3.  **Join a support group**: Connect with others who have experienced similar situations, either online or in-person.\n4.  **Reach out to HR or a supervisor**: Document incidents and report any discriminatory behavior to ensure your rights are protected.\n5.  **Prioritize self-care**: Engage in activities that bring you joy, practice relaxation techniques, and take care of your physical health.\n\nYou're not alone, and there are people who care about you and want to help. Don't hesitate to reach out for support. You deserve to work in a safe and respectful environment.",
